---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJson from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJson.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-escape">
  <DevFormatJson client:load mode="escape" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What escaping for JSON means</h2>
    <p>
      A JSON string is delimited by double quotes, so a double quote inside it must be marked as data,
      not a delimiter. The same applies to the backslash that does the marking, and to the control
      characters that cannot appear literally. JSON escape applies those rules to any text, so the
      result is one valid JSON string.
    </p>

    <h2>The complete escape table</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Escaped as</th><th>Required?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Double quote <code>"</code></td><td><code>\"</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Backslash <code>\</code></td><td><code>\\</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Backspace (U+0008)</td><td><code>\b</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Form feed (U+000C)</td><td><code>\f</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Line feed (U+000A)</td><td><code>\n</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Carriage return (U+000D)</td><td><code>\r</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tab (U+0009)</td><td><code>\t</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Other control characters</td><td><code>\u00XX</code></td><td>Always</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Forward slash <code>/</code></td><td><code>\/</code></td><td>Optional. Needed only to avoid <code>&lt;/script&gt;</code> inside HTML</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Any printable Unicode</td><td>Itself</td><td>Never. JSON is UTF-8</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The nested-JSON case</h2>
    <p>
      The most common use is to put one JSON document inside another. Webhook payloads do it
      (<code>&#123;"event": "x", "payload": "&#123;\"id\":1&#125;"&#125;</code>), log aggregators do it,
      and so do message queues that carry a typed envelope around an opaque body. Paste the inner
      document here, keep the surrounding quotes, and put the result into the outer document as a value.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you escape twice, you get <code>\\"</code> everywhere. This is almost always a bug in a pipeline
      where two layers both escape. If the text has more backslashes than quotes, run
      <a href="/json-unescape">unescape</a> until it stops changing, then fix the layer that escapes
      without need.
    </p>

    <h2>Escaping is context-specific</h2>
    <p>
      People often confuse three escaping schemes. Each makes text &ldquo;safe&rdquo; for one context
      only.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>JSON escaping uses backslashes and applies inside a JSON string.</li>
      <li>URL encoding uses <code>%XX</code> and applies inside a URL. See the <a href="/url-encoder-decoder">URL encoder</a>.</li>
      <li>HTML escaping uses <code>&amp;entity;</code> and applies inside markup. See the <a href="/html-entity-encoder">HTML entity encoder</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Text that goes through two contexts needs both, in the correct order. Escape JSON in an HTML
      attribute for JSON first, then for HTML.
    </p>

    <h2>Should the quotes be included?</h2>
    <p>
      Keep them when you paste the result as a complete JSON value. Turn them off when the destination
      already has quotes, for example between the quotes of an existing string in your editor.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
